    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? goblins?? lol. anyway, what did you mean about the caching part? like how does that actually work to make it slow? is it because of some setting in the record itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The goblins, they're just a metaphor for the complexity of the DNS system, I assure you. Now, about caching, it's a bit like a group of goblins who have memorized a map of the forest, but they're not always up to date. When a DNS server caches a record, it's essentially storing a copy of the record in its memory, so it can quickly look up the information without having to query the original DNS server.\n\nHowever, this caching process can lead to delays in propagating changes, because the cached records can take time to expire and be replaced with the new information. It's a bit like the goblins having to relearn the map every time it changes - they need to update their knowledge to reflect the new path.\n\nIn terms of settings, caching is typically controlled by the DNS server itself, and it can be configured to cache records for a certain amount of time. Some DNS servers may also use techniques like \"time-to-live\" (TTL) to control how long a record is cached. A lower TTL means the record will be updated more frequently, while a higher TTL means it will be cached for longer.\n\nFor example, if you set the TTL for a DNS record to 300",
      "source": "target",
